## AWS Workload Identity Federation

Organizations configured for AWS Workload Identity Federation can authenticate
without Datadog API or application keys and without passing AWS credentials to
Terraform:

```terraform
provider "datadog" {
  org_uuid            = var.datadog_org_uuid
  cloud_provider_type = "aws"
}
```

The provider uses the AWS SDK default credential chain. This includes standard
sources such as environment credentials, shared configuration and credential
files, IAM Identity Center (SSO), web identity, ECS task roles, EC2 instance
roles, and assume-role profiles. For example, after logging in locally with
`aws sso login --profile sandbox`, run Terraform with `AWS_PROFILE=sandbox`.

Explicit `aws_access_key_id`, `aws_secret_access_key`, and `aws_session_token`
values remain supported but are not required. Workload Identity Federation must
be enabled for the Datadog organization, and its identity or intake mapping must
match the AWS identity resolved by the credential chain.

## Terraform Dynamic Provider Credentials

For HCP Terraform, the provider discovers Terraform-issued workload identity tokens and exchanges them through Datadog Workload Identity Federation when authentication is not explicitly configured in the provider block. Use `workload_identity_token_tag` to select a distinct token for each provider configuration. Configure the WIF persona mapping and workspace audience as described in the [Dynamic Provider Credentials guide](guides/dynamic_provider_credentials.md).

## Authenticate with pup

For local development, the provider can use the OAuth session from the [pup](https://github.com/DataDog/pup) CLI, so you don't need to create or store an API key, application key, or access token.

**Prerequisites**: Install pup, make sure it's on your `PATH`, and run `pup auth login`.

To turn it on, set `use_pup = true` in the provider block, or set the `DD_USE_PUP` environment variable to `true`. Also set `org_uuid`, which is required with pup, so every token must belong to that organization:

```terraform
provider "datadog" {
  use_pup  = true
  org_uuid = "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
}
```

The provider gets a token by running `pup auth token`, and requests a new one when it expires, so long-running applies stay authenticated.

The provider uses pup only when no other credentials are configured. API and application keys, `bearer_token`, cloud provider authentication, and HCP Terraform dynamic provider credentials all take precedence, whether they're set in the provider block or in environment variables.

- **Changing pup accounts during a run**: If pup is logged in as a different user partway through a run, the provider fails at the next token request instead of continuing as that user. Because `org_uuid` is required, a token for a different organization also stops the run.
- **Site matching**: Set `api_url` to the same Datadog site that pup is logged in to.
- **Parallel runs**: Each Terraform run calls `pup auth token` on its own. If several runs try to refresh an expired session at the same time, the provider retries a failed `pup auth token` call up to three times before returning an error.

